{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310614,"nonce":"m/QO3po3FNe4dh/nwYsvsgiRnDo=","title":"FIXED 2d7c200a — an un-annotated method's return type decayed to Dynamic when the caller was declared ABOVE it; the 71-row `uncompiled` ...","message":"**THE BUG.** An un-annotated return type is inferred WHILE THAT METHOD'S BODY\nIS LOWERED, and bodies lower in DECLARATION ORDER. So a caller written ABOVE\nthe callee resolved the return as Dynamic — the same source meant different\nthings depending on member order.\n\n**MINIMAL REPRO** (kept in the scratchpad as RET5/RET6): identical class, only\nthe order of `parseToks` and the constructor swapped. Declared-first = works;\ndeclared-after = `E0801: ambiguous dynamic method dispatch: isEmpty ... the\nreceiver's type is unresolved`. NOTE the candidate list is VecBool/VecF64/\nVecI32/VecI64/VecPtr/Channel — `List` is not even a candidate, which is the\ntell that the receiver decayed rather than mis-resolved.\n\n**FIXED 2d7c200a** in the PRE-REGISTRATION pass\n(`ast_lowering/decls/classes.rs`, \"PRE-REGISTER ALL METHODS\"). That pass already\nhad `anonymous_return_type_from_ast` for the same reason — its own comment\ndocuments the order-dependence. Added `constructed_return_type_from_ast`:\n`return new C\u003cT\u003e(...)`, or `return v` for a local declared `var v = new C\u003cT\u003e()`.\n\n**ONLY `new C\u003cT\u003e(...)` WITH TYPE ARGUMENTS QUALIFIES — MEASURED.** Typing a\nplain `new C(...)` too CRASHED 3 Haxe-suite tests (DebugChecksum,\ndebug_checksum, mandelbrot_small; `fault=0xc004000000000008` = a float bit\npattern used as a pointer). The class need not be registered yet at\npre-registration time, and those returns already worked as Dynamic. **The Haxe\nsuite caught this and conformance did NOT** — 502 either way. Exactly\n[[feedback_conformance_alone_hides_regressions]].\n\n**RESULT: `uncompiled` rows 71 -\u003e 66, conformance 502 -\u003e 502.** All 5\n`uncompiled haxe.Template.new` rows cleared the compile failure but do NOT pass\nyet — they now hit the NEXT defect in the chain.\n\n**THE CHAIN BEHIND Template, in order:**\n1. (fixed) forward-declared return decays to Dynamic.\n2. (OPEN) `List\u003cT\u003e.first()` returns the erased T WITHOUT unboxing — prints an\n   ADDRESS instead of the element. `/tmp/probe/LST1.hx`. Same family as\n   [[bugs_erased_null_t_box_at_type_param_boundary]].\n3. (OPEN) reading a field off that value faults at `0x13`.\n\n**THE `uncompiled` CLUSTER IS THE LEVER, 66 ROWS.** \"uncompiled X\" = a FORWARD\nSTUB: `is_forward_stub()` is a CFG SHAPE (one empty block ending in\n`Unreachable`, ir/functions.rs), not a flag, reported by\n`rayzor_uncompiled_function`. Sub-clusters worth mining: doubled class names\n(`unit.issues.A.A.field`, `unit.MyAbstract.MyAbstract.foo`), an uncompiled\nRUNTIME function (`haxe_string_char_code_at_ptr`), `Bytes.getData`, `__init__`,\nand a monomorphized name (`set_value__i32`).\n\n**METHOD THAT WORKED:** take the named function from the row, reproduce it in a\nstandalone probe, and BISECT AGAINST THE REAL STDLIB FILE. The verbatim body\npassed standalone — only re-ordering it reproduced the failure, which is what\nidentified declaration order as the variable. Annotating the return type on a\nthrowaway copy of the stdlib file CONFIRMED the cause in one run (revert it\nimmediately — [[feedback_stdlib_source_stays_upstream_shaped]]).\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): FIXED 2d7c200a: an un-annotated return decayed to Dynamic when the CALLER was declared ABOVE the callee (order-dependent!). Only `new C\u003cT\u003e()` may be recovered — typing plain `new C()` crashed 3 suite tests that conformance did NOT catch. NEXT LEVER: the 66-row `uncompiled` (forward-stub) cluster.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_forward_declared_return_type_decays` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}